{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1530107780,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE3MDM5NjQ4NTY="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1530108757,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwMDY4NjMwNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2673#issuecomment-400686307"},"message":"\u003e ATM datalad remove would also drop the content. In my usecase, I just want git rm but with all our paths handling across subdatasets\n\nThe content isn't dropped if `--nocheck` is used.  Since you're after the same behavior as `git rm ...`, the fact that a check isn't performed should be fine, right?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1530117875,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwMDc0ODgwNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2673#issuecomment-400748807"},"message":"So I think that the logic then needs to be fixed up as well.  I see (and read the doc of) `--nocheck` as a flag for `drop` action. And I think that is what it should be, it should not decide between `drop` and `no-drop`.  What do you think @mih?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a42670b7cb1bea674eb6caba566df2b63d954364"},"timestamp":1530898009,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwMzA5Nzk3OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2673#issuecomment-403097978"},"message":"I just ran into this (trying to do the equivalent of `git rm` on a data set and settling on the `--nocheck` flag). It's nice to know that that seems like the best solution at present, but just to +1 @yarikoptic's comment: it'd be great to either better document this or to have a more traditional option for those of us used to `git rm` behavior !","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1530910365,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwMzE0Mzk4OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2673#issuecomment-403143989"},"message":"This was and is a complicated topic, hence the unsatisfactory situation. Which is even more complicated and unsatisfactory than what is described in this issue.\n\nWe need to consider that remove not just operates on files, but also on subdatasets. It is save to always git rm any file, because nothing is lost, and we can easily undo this action. The same isnt true for subdatasets. If we rm them, we rm the entire thing including its annex. If we do not drop in this case, we will see very unfortunate things being reported ;-)\n\nSo what do you want remove --no-drop to do in case of a subdataset? Just rm -rf ?\n\nWhile thinking about this, I want to re remind the pending discussion on changing the location of the .git dir of subdatasets to lie within the parent and not within the subdataset, which would mitigate the problem to some degree.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1530913070,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwMzE1MzEyOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2673#issuecomment-403153128"},"message":"Just a note that IMHO changing location of storage to parent dataset would only complicate things, since now we would need to have even 1 more scenario where you would really remove it also from parent. Once again, imho, now we could just refuse to remove subdataset if --no-drop is provided and subdataset contains some annexed load. For regular files it seems to be a no brainier overall","files":null}]}